    The feasibility of real-time calculation of parameters for an internal
  combustion engine via reconfigurable hardware implementation is investigated
  as an alternative to software computation. A detailed in-hardware field
  programmable gate array (FPGA)-based design is developed and evaluated using
  input crank angle and in-cylinder pressure data from fully instrumented
  diesel engines in the QUT Biofuel Engine Research Facility (BERF). Results
  indicate the feasibility of employing a hardware-based implementation for
  real-time processing for speeds comparable to the data sampling rate
  currently used in the facility, with acceptably low level of discrepancies
  between hardware and software-based calculation of key engine parameters.%
